SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillets: the blend cut by the face at the spine's end (0059) where the
narrow face vanishes: along the middle of the chain the contact runs along
the face's edge with the face at the end, towards the ends past it
(mitcad#121): e.g. the foot of a groove's end wall where the groove's
bottom is exactly as deep as the rounding and rounds up into the part's
side at both sides.

- ChFi3d_Builder::SplitKPart: a tangency line along the boundary of its
  face can leave the hatching undone (Geom2dHatch_Hatcher::IsDone false)
  instead of giving no domain; it is hatched on the boundary then too
  (HatchOnBoundary, 0014), as where no domain is found.
- ChFi3d_KPartCutToEnd (0059): the piece nearest to the end may be such a
  piece on the boundary: its contact on N runs along an edge E of N whose
  other face is the face B at the spine's end, and its end lies on E (no
  arc given). Its end point is given the arc E (the vertex too where it
  lies at one), and the pieces cut by B run from there to the end. Where
  the pieces are not added, the point is restored.
- ChFi3d_ContactsAlongFaceEdges (0014, 0054): an inner end of a contact
  along a part of an edge, next to a piece whose contact runs off N onto
  the face beyond Fv (cut by it), puts its point on the edge with an
  external transition, as the neighbour's point there: N keeps no piece
  beyond the inner end either, so no part of the edge is kept for N. With
  the transitions of a contact leaving N and coming back, the parts of the
  edge beyond the inner ends stayed with Fv's boundary, or (reversed) the
  part along the blend came twice into Fv's wire.
